# py_console.ari — a REUSABLE Python console: a scrollback, an input line, and Clear, over the
# scripting hosts' console natives (ARIADNE.md §9.6). It is plain widgets, so ariglx (ImGui), arix
# (the terminal) and ariglx-web (the browser) all draw it; ImGui's input line has a Run button, and
# arix runs the cell with Enter. The stock hosts have no Python, so the console needs one of those.
#
# The document turns its console on in its python: block, and each cell runs in the namespace that
# block ran in (its names are the cell's, and a name one cell sets is there for the next):
#     requires: [python]
#     python:
#       console: true
#       source: |
#         import math
#     import: cvc://components/py_console.ari
#     windows:
#       - include: py_console
#         args: { id: console, title: Python }
#         pos: [20, 40]
#         size: [520, 360]
# An import doesn't carry this file's needs: the document's own requires: and python: do.
#
# Import the file for its units and the functions behind them:
#   py_console        a window holding the panel
#       args: id (py_console), title (Python)
#   py_console_panel  just the widgets, to embed in a document's own window
#       args: id (py_console)
# `id` names the console: its state lives under <id>.*, its window's under tree.<id>.*. Consoles
# with different ids keep their own scrollback, and share the document's namespace.
#
# Enter (or Run, under ImGui) runs the cell; Shift+Enter (Alt+Enter or Ctrl+J in a terminal) adds a
# line to it. A cell runs as a notebook runs one: its prints and its last expression's value are
# shown, input() raises EOFError, an exception shows its traceback, and SystemExit ends the cell,
# not the document. A cell runs on the frame's thread until it ends, so a long one holds the frame.
#
# The functions (any program of the document may call them):
#   (py-console-run ID SOURCE)    run SOURCE as console ID's next cell: {ok output log}, and the
#                                 scrollback at ID.log
#   (py-console-clear ID)         empty console ID's scrollback
#
# State, with C = the include's id:
#   C.log      the scrollback: each cell after >>> (and ... for its other lines), then its output
#   C.draft    the cell being written (the message_input's bind)

functions: |
  (defun py-console-run (c source)
    (set r (python-console c source))
    (state-set (str-concat c ".log") (get-attr r "log"))
    r)
  (defun py-console-clear (c)
    (python-console-clear c)
    (state-set (str-concat c ".log") ""))

units:
  py_console:
    args: { id: py_console, title: Python }
    window: "{title}"
    id: "{id}"
    closable: true
    children:
      - include: py_console_panel
        args: { id: "{id}" }

  py_console_panel:
    args: { id: py_console }
    type: group
    id: "{id}_panel"
    children:
      # The scrollback fills the window less what is under it, and follows the newest output.
      - text_view: "##{id}_log"
        bind: "{id}.log"
      - message_input: "##{id}_in"
        bind: "{id}.draft"
        hint: ">>> Python (Shift+Enter: a new line)"
        max_lines: 8
        send: Run
        on: (py-console-run "{id}" (get-attr event "text"))
      - button: Clear
        on: (py-console-clear "{id}")
